<div class="body" id="fhbody-186502022">
	

	
		
		<div id="text-186502022" class="p">
			
		 	
				Slashdot readers <a href="/~Cpt_Kirks">Cpt_Kirks</a>, <a href="https://slashdot.org/~eklektic">eklektic</a>, <a href="https://slashdot.org/~sconeu">sconeu</a>, and <a href="https://slashdot.org/~Vrallis">Vrallis</a> share the unfortunate news of Patrick Stewart's passing. Longtime spokesperson Kelly Bush Novak said Stewart <a href="https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/patrick-stewart-dead-star-trek-x-men-1236742932/">died Wednesday at the age of 86</a> at his home in Los Angeles following "a brief and poignant journey with Alzheimer's disease and Lewy body dementia."
<br> <br>
"He has been held in the warm embrace of family and friends, surrounded by live music, prayer, tears, laughter and the kind of profound emotion that only he could inspire after a lifetime of devotion to us all," Bush Novak wrote. "We will forever cherish the peaceful ease and reflection on his face, an enduring reminder of his invincible grace."
<br> <br>
The acclaimed British actor became a <a href="https://entertainment.slashdot.org/story/09/12/31/1322251/sir-patrick-stewart">science-fiction icon</a> as Capt. Jean-Luc Picard in Star Trek: The Next Generation and <a href="https://news.slashdot.org/story/00/07/17/0016204/slashdot-meets-x-men">Professor Charles Xavier</a> in the X-Men films. Beyond those iconic roles, Stewart earned multiple Emmy and Golden Globe nominations for performances in Moby Dick, The Lion in Winter, Extras, Hamlet, and Blunt Talk. He later <a href="https://entertainment.slashdot.org/story/19/07/21/2014201/comic-con-trailers-include-star-trek-picard-and-hbos-watchmen-series">returned</a> to his most famous role in Star Trek: Picard.<br>
		 	
