	<h2 class="story">
		

		

		
		

		

		

		

		<span id="title-186491996" class="story-title"> <a onclick="return toggle_fh_body_wrap_return(this);" href="//tech.slashdot.org/story/26/10/29/052237/please-let-the-internet-die">'Please Let the Internet Die'</a> <span class=" no extlnk"><a class="story-sourcelnk" href="https://www.wired.com/story/let-the-internet-die/" title="External link - https://www.wired.com/story/let-the-internet-die/" target="_blank"> (wired.com) </a></span></span>

		
		
		<!--<span class="comments commentcnt-186491996" >137</span>-->
		

		
		<!-- comment bubble -->
		
			<span class="comment-bubble"><a href="//tech.slashdot.org/story/26/10/29/052237/please-let-the-internet-die#comments" title="">137</a></span>
		
	</h2>
	<div class="details" id="details-186491996">
		<span class="story-details">
		<span class="story-views">
			<span class="sodify" onclick="firehose_set_options('color', 'red')" title="Filter Firehose to entries rated red or better"></span><span class="icon-beaker pop1 " alt="Popularity" title="Filter Firehose to entries rated red or better" onclick="firehose_set_options('color', 'red')"><span></span></span> 
		</span>
		</span>
		<span class="story-byline">
	
				
			Posted
				by 
		
		
			
				  <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/beauhd/" rel="nofollow">BeauHD</a>
			
		
		

		
		
		<time id="fhtime-186491996" datetime="on Thursday October 29, 2026 @11:00AM">on Thursday October 29, 2026 @11:00AM</time>
		
		
			 from the <span class="dept-text">time-to-start-over</span> dept.
		
		</span>
	</div>
</header>

<div class="body" id="fhbody-186491996">
	

	
		
		<div id="text-186491996" class="p">
			
		 	
				Slashdot reader <a href="/~fjo3">fjo3</a> shares an article from Wired: <i> The internet <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/let-the-internet-die/">must be put out of its misery</a>. Any rational observer can admit that the current iteration of the web -- and the social fabric woven there -- is not what they signed up for. Symptoms of advanced cultural decay can no longer be ignored. This thing is eating itself. Giants including Meta and Google have been forced to <a href="https://yro.slashdot.org/story/26/08/26/1632251/meta-reaches-18-billion-of-settlements-over-childrens-social-media-addiction">settle massive consumer lawsuits</a> brought over <a href="https://yro.slashdot.org/story/26/01/26/2222213/google-settles-68-million-lawsuit-claiming-it-recorded-private-conversations">privacy violations</a> and <a href="https://meta.slashdot.org/story/26/03/25/1745232/meta-and-youtube-found-negligent-in-landmark-social-media-addiction-case">mental health harms</a> allegedly caused by the addictive mechanisms of their platforms. Schools are <a href="https://mobile.slashdot.org/story/26/04/20/2014246/mobile-phones-to-be-banned-in-schools-in-england-under-new-plans">banning smartphones</a> as multiple countries institute <a href="https://tech.slashdot.org/story/25/12/09/2112230/millions-of-australian-teens-lose-access-to-social-media-as-ban-takes-effect">social media restrictions</a> for under-16s. Parents are in a panic about their kids aspiring to <a href="https://tech.slashdot.org/story/26/07/27/0238219/a-new-middle-class-of-content-creators-is-quietly-quitting-the-9-to-5">careers as social media celebrities</a>, losing themselves in a vast ecosystem of <a href="https://tech.slashdot.org/story/25/07/30/205208/scammers-unleash-flood-of-slick-online-gaming-sites">online casinos</a>, or <a href="https://yro.slashdot.org/story/17/05/07/0126206/social-media-giants-sued-for-helping-isis">joining extremist movements</a>. Generative AI, briefly considered an amusing novelty, is now a <a href="https://tech.slashdot.org/story/26/03/17/2243219/ai-job-loss-research-ignores-how-ai-is-utterly-destroying-the-internet">turbocharged slop engine</a> for the web, filling feeds and comment sections with <a href="https://tech.slashdot.org/story/25/08/23/022223/making-cash-off-ai-slop-the-surreal-video-business-taking-over-the-web">useless, grotesque content</a>. As humans scroll past headlines about <a href="https://slashdot.org/story/26/08/25/1756243/ai-is-hitting-entry-level-jobs-hardest-stanford-study-finds">AI taking their jobs</a>, confusing fantasy for reality, and <a href="https://science.slashdot.org/story/26/04/03/2334204/cognitive-surrender-leads-ai-users-to-abandon-logical-thinking-research-finds">contributing to cognitive decline</a>, any consumer appeal the technology might have is turning to anguish. The data centers that power it have become <a href="https://news.slashdot.org/story/26/05/15/0217208/americans-would-rather-have-a-nuclear-plant-in-their-backyard-than-a-datacenter">increasingly unpopular</a>, and <a href="https://politics.slashdot.org/story/26/08/20/1951246/voters-arent-waiting-for-november-to-try-ousting-officials-over-data-centers">politically radioactive</a>.
<br> <br>
To be online, many now admit, is to be endlessly manipulated and exploited. In December, Oxford University Press <a href="https://tech.slashdot.org/story/25/12/06/026245/rage-bait-named-oxford-word-of-the-year-2025">declared</a> rage bait to be the word of the year, noting that use of the term had tripled over the previous 12 months. The lexical spike represented a heightened awareness that monetized social accounts <a href="https://politics.slashdot.org/story/26/08/19/1623200/xs-algorithm-feeds-off-ragebait-and-impacts-democrats-more-study-finds">churn out bad-faith takes and extremist content</a>, automating this abusive behavior with generative AI, simply to <a href="https://tech.slashdot.org/story/25/09/09/0048216/sam-altman-says-bots-are-making-social-media-feel-fake">profit off hostile engagement</a>. Billionaire oligarchs have opened the floodgates to such inauthentic activity, conspiracy-mongering, and <a href="https://tech.slashdot.org/story/26/06/10/1748245/threats-against-politicians-tripled-after-meta-changed-its-speech-rules">hate speech by rolling back moderation</a>. In short, when you hang around online in 2026, you see little worth saving. Perhaps a decade ago, you felt that the glimmers of community and joy, of artistic and intellectual exchange, were enough to balance out the toxic junk. No longer.</i><br>
		 	
		</div>

		

		

		
